---
import BaseLayoutV2 from '../layouts/BaseLayoutV2.astro';
import EmailCaptureForm from '../components/EmailCaptureForm.tsx';

const questions = [
  { n: '1', q: 'What exactly are you deciding, and what triggered it now?', body: 'State the decision in one sentence, and name what made it live today rather than last month.' },
  { n: '2', q: 'What is the decision underneath this one?', body: 'People often ask about the surface choice while avoiding the real one. If this decision went away, what larger question would still be sitting there?' },
  { n: '3', q: 'What is the false binary?', body: 'You have probably framed this as A or B. Name a third option you have not been treating as real, including doing nothing, or a path that is neither A nor B.' },
  { n: '4', q: 'What is the one assumption the whole thing rests on?', body: 'Find the single belief that, if it turned out to be wrong, would change everything. Not a list of risks. The load-bearing one.' },
  { n: '5', q: 'Which of your "facts" are actually assumptions?', body: 'Take the constraints you are treating as fixed (a deadline, a budget, a flat no) and mark each one: verified, or just assumed?' },
  { n: '6', q: 'What do you actually know?', body: 'For each thing the decision turns on, tag it: given (you know it for a fact), inferred (you are reasoning it), or unknown (you do not know, and it matters).' },
];
---
<BaseLayoutV2
  title="The Decision Frame | GreenSquare"
  description="A free one-page tool that interrogates your decision before you evaluate anything. Six questions, about ten minutes."
  current="frame"
>
  <!-- HERO — light, clean -->
  <section class="v-section v-off hero">
    <div class="v-container v-container--narrow v-reveal">
      <h1 class="v-display">The Decision Frame.</h1>
      <p class="v-lead" style="margin-top: var(--v-6);">
        Most free tools, prompt packs, decision matrices, pros-and-cons templates, jump straight to weighing
        options. None of them check whether you are deciding the right thing. The Frame does the one move they
        skip: it interrogates the decision first, so you do not run a rigorous process on the wrong question.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="v-btn v-btn--green" href="#get-it">Get the free frame</a>
        <a class="v-btn v-btn--grey" href="/product/">See the full Decision</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW TO USE IT -->
  <section class="v-section">
    <div class="v-container v-container--narrow v-reveal">
      <p class="v-measure">
        Run it on a real decision you are facing. Work through it by hand, or paste the six questions into
        Claude or GPT with your decision and ask it to work through them in order. Answer honestly, the value is
        in the questions you would rather skip. It takes about ten minutes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THE SIX QUESTIONS — indexed capability list -->
  <section class="v-section v-fill">
    <div class="v-container">
      <h2 class="v-h2" style="max-width: 20ch;">The six questions.</h2>
      <div class="v-caplist">
        {questions.map((item) => (
          <div class="v-caprow v-reveal">
            <span class="v-index">/{item.n.padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p class="v-small v-cap-body">{item.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHAT YOU GET — worksheet card -->
  <section class="v-section">
    <div class="v-container v-container--narrow">
      <h2 class="v-h2">What you get.</h2>
      <p class="v-measure" style="margin-top: var(--v-4);">When you have worked through the six, you will have four things:</p>
      <div class="v-card v-reveal" style="margin-top: var(--v-6);">
        <ul class="v-leadlist">
          <li><strong>The real decision.</strong> Reframed, often not the one you started with.</li>
          <li><strong>The false binary, exposed.</strong> With the third option you had not been treating as real.</li>
          <li><strong>The load-bearing assumption.</strong> The single belief the whole call rests on.</li>
          <li><strong>An honest evidence map.</strong> What you know, marked given, inferred or unknown.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <hr class="v-rule" style="margin-block: var(--v-8);" />
      <p class="v-measure">
        You are now framed. You are not yet decided. Framing is the front half of the work. The deciding
        (generating genuine options, weighing the evidence, an evidence-tagged recommendation, and a self-check
        before you commit) is the other half. That half is <a href="/product/">the Decision</a>, the paid
        GreenSquare product. For now, use the Frame. It is yours, and it works on its own.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- GET IT — green big-block, CTA -->
  <section class="v-section v-green" id="get-it">
    <div class="v-container v-split is-top">
      <div class="v-reveal">
        <h2 class="v-h2">Send me the free Decision Frame.</h2>
        <p class="v-lead" style="margin-top: var(--v-5);">
          A one-page PDF, delivered by email. Double opt-in: confirm your address and the Frame arrives straight
          after.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="v-reveal d1">
        <div class="v-card">
          <EmailCaptureForm client:visible buttonLabel="Send me the free Decision Frame" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayoutV2>

<style>
  .hero { padding-top: clamp(3rem, 6vh, 5rem); }
  .hero-actions { display: flex; gap: var(--v-6); align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: var(--v-8); }
</style>
